<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<chapter xml:id="ch3-chapter-model-and-monte-carlo" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Monte Carlo Simulation</title>

  <introduction>
    <p> 
      This chapter introduces students to the idea of building and simulating models, which will later be used to perform
      simulation-based inference. This chapter will also cover very basic probability ideas and will begin 
      to teach students how to summarize and describe distributions of data (single variable). There will also be a section on sampling distributions, sampling variability, and standard error.  
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <objectives>
    <ol>
    <li>Explore ideas of randomness, which permeates, and is fundamental to statistics. Specifically, understand sampling variability and standard error.</li>

    <li>Learn to model random processes and generate outcomes from these models/simulate data from these models.</li>

    <li>Summarize simulation results after generating multiple outcomes from the same model, which helps us to make sense of the process we are modeling. Specifically, we will summarize data using graphs and statistics when we have one variable of interest.</li>

    <li>Use Monte Carlo simulation to estimate the probability of an event of interest occurring.</li>
  </ol>
  </objectives>
  
